# NOTE: a `.pubignore` *replaces* `.gitignore` for the directory it sits in --
# it does not add to it. So everything the root `.gitignore` excludes has to be
# repeated here, or build output ships in the archive.

# --- Mirrors .gitignore -----------------------------------------------------
*.class
*.log
*.pyc
*.swp
.DS_Store
.atom/
.build/
.buildlog/
.history
.svn/
.swiftpm/
migrate_working_dir/

*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
.idea/

# NOTE: no leading slash on `build/` and `coverage/`, deliberately -- the root
# `.gitignore` anchors them because git only needs the root ones. An unanchored
# pattern matches at any depth, which is what keeps `example/build` (2.4 GB
# after one `flutter run`) and `example/.dart_tool` (another 605 MB) out of the
# archive without depending on `example/.gitignore` staying where it is. The
# failure mode here is a multi-gigabyte upload, not a warning.
pubspec.lock
**/doc/api/
.dart_tool/
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
build/
coverage/

node_modules/
bun.lock

# Integration-test fixtures, written by `dart run tool/dump_fixtures.dart`.
/example/assets/

# --- Published-archive only -------------------------------------------------
# Commit tooling: commitlint and lefthook run in this repo, never in a
# consumer's build.
/package.json
/commitlint.config.cjs
/lefthook.yml

# Contributor tooling -- the WASM build script, the fixture dumper and the
# cross-binding digest checker. All three are things CI and maintainers run
# against `src/`, and none of them mean anything from inside a pub cache.
#
# `src/` and `assets/monowave.wasm` DO ship, and must: `hook/build.dart`
# compiles the former during a *consumer's* build, and the latter is the web
# binding's half of the same core. Excluding either breaks every install.
/tool/
# Website-only: HTML specimens embedded into the docs site, useless in a package.
/demos/

# The guides. They live on the docs site, which the pubspec points at, and in
# the repo, which the README links into -- so shipping a second copy inside the
# archive gains a consumer nothing.
#
# It also has to go, rather than merely being tidy: pub's layout convention
# wants a singular `doc/`, and `pub publish` treats the plural as a warning,
# which makes `--dry-run` exit 65 and fails the validate gate in
# `.github/workflows/publish.yml`. Renaming would drag the `docs/**` trigger in
# `docs-sync.yml`, the README's links and the docs site's own paths along with
# it, to put files in the archive nobody reads from there.
/docs/
